# Ownership boundaries by directory — GitHub suggests (and, once branch
# protection is turned on to require it, enforces) review from whoever
# owns a path a PR touches. Last matching pattern wins, same rule as
# .gitignore, so put broader patterns first and narrower overrides after.
#
# Everything defaults to @TarunVishwakarma1 today (solo repo) — the point
# of splitting these lines now, rather than one catch-all, is that handing
# a piece to someone else later is a one-line edit here, not a restructure.
# Chosen as the CODEOWNERS-only alternative to physically splitting
# web/apps/{web,docs,dashboard} into separate repos: they share a live
# design system (packages/ui) that a real repo split would force behind a
# publish step, which isn't worth it for three lightweight Next.js apps.

*                           @TarunVishwakarma1

# backend/ and ios/truelable/ are git submodules — this only covers the
# pointer bump in this repo, not their own commit history (each has its
# own repo and could carry its own CODEOWNERS if that ever matters):
#   https://github.com/TarunVishwakarma1/true-label-backend
#   https://github.com/TarunVishwakarma1/true-label-ios
/backend                    @TarunVishwakarma1
/ios/truelable              @TarunVishwakarma1

/web/apps/web/              @TarunVishwakarma1
/web/apps/docs/             @TarunVishwakarma1
/web/apps/dashboard/        @TarunVishwakarma1

# Shared by all three apps above (design tokens, theme-toggle, providers)
# — a change here ripples into every app's build, so it's worth a
# deliberate owner even before there's a second person to hand it to.
/web/packages/               @TarunVishwakarma1

/k8s/                        @TarunVishwakarma1
/infra/                      @TarunVishwakarma1
/deploy/                     @TarunVishwakarma1

# CI/CD and repo automation — changes here affect every branch's pipeline.
/.github/                    @TarunVishwakarma1
